  "slug": "microtubule-kinesin-e1e6",
  "summary": "Recent preprints define how lattice compaction, MAP7D1 recognition and a designed miniprotein tune kinesin-1 engagement with microtubules.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how microtubule lattice state and microtubule-associated proteins control kinesin-1 access. A preprint reports that doublecortin compacts lattices through its DC1 domain, stiffening microtubules and inhibiting kinesin-1 and TAT1 binding, based on in vitro and simulation work. Another preprint shows that kinesin-1 preferentially moves along stable microtubules through MAP7D1, which recognizes expanded lattice conformations. A third introduces Sidetrak, a de novo-designed 36-residue miniprotein that binds the kinesin-1 coiled-coil stalk, blocks MAP7 binding and disrupts mitochondrial transport in cells. Together these studies position the kinesin-1 stalk and lattice conformation as regulatory control points. The C. elegans EFA-6 preprint and the ESCRT-III membrane repair study address distinct processes and are not part of this thread.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
